{
  "dom_id": "step-151114",
  "name": "Squaring Negative Numbers",
  "problem": "The squares of negative numbers are always positive because a negative times a negative is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-)\\,\\cdot \\,(-)\\,=\\,(+)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome squares of negative numbers are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}\\,=\\,(-1)\\cdot (-1) & \\,=\\,1 \\\\ (-2)^{2}\\,=\\,(-2)\\cdot (-2) & \\,=\\,4 \\\\ (-3)^{2}\\,=\\,(-3)\\cdot (-3) & \\,=\\,9 \\\\ (-4)^{2}\\,=\\,(-4)\\cdot (-4) & \\,=\\,16 \\\\ (-5)^{2}\\,=\\,(-5)\\cdot (-5) & \\,=\\,25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** When squaring negative numbers, the parentheses are important! \n\nConsider the following squared quantity:\n\n$$\n-3^2\n$$\n\nThis is *not* the same as $(-3)^2$. Instead, this is a shorthand way of writing\n\n$$\n(-1) \\cdot 3^2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the order of operations, we should square $3$ *first* and *then* multiply by $-1.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{3}^{2} & =(-1)\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
